<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-probability-transforms">
<title>Probability Transforms</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Random variables, discrete and continuous distributions, expectation,
moments, functions of random variables, infinite series, power series,
complex numbers, improper integrals, derivatives, and basic transform
methods.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>explain why probability transforms are useful;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define probability generating functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recover probabilities from probability generating functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute factorial moments from probability generating
          functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define moment generating functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute moments from moment generating functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use moment generating functions to identify distributions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>derive transforms of sums of independent random variables;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define characteristic functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>explain why characteristic functions always exist;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute characteristic functions for standard distributions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand uniqueness of characteristic functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use characteristic functions for sums and convergence;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define Laplace transforms of nonnegative random variables;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>connect Laplace transforms with moment generating functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define cumulant generating functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute basic cumulants;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand additivity of cumulants for independent sums;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish raw moments, factorial moments, and cumulants;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use transforms to derive standard distributional closure
          properties;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize transforms as algebraic encodings of probability
          distributions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand transform inversion conceptually;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>connect probability transforms with generating functions,
          Fourier analysis, asymptotic probability, and stochastic
          processes.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-why-probability-transforms">
<title>Why Use Probability Transforms?</title>
<p>A probability distribution can often be encoded inside another
function.</p>

<p>This may convert difficult probability operations into simpler
algebraic operations.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{convolution of distributions}
}</md>
</p>

<p>often becomes</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{multiplication of transforms}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly, moments can often be recovered from derivatives of a
transform.</p>

<p>Thus probability transforms provide a bridge between</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{probability}
+
\text{algebra}
+
\text{analysis}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-main-probability-transforms">
<title>Main Probability Transforms</title>
<p>Important transforms include:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{probability generating function}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{moment generating function}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{characteristic function}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Laplace transform}
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{cumulant generating function}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Each transform is useful in a different setting.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-probability-generating-functions">
<title>Probability Generating Functions</title>
<p>Probability generating functions are particularly useful for
nonnegative integer-valued random variables.</p>

<definition>
<title>Probability Generating Function</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>X</m> take values in</p>

<p>
<md>\{0,1,2,\ldots\}.</md>
</p>

<p>The probability generating function, abbreviated PGF, is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(s)
=
\mathbb{E}[s^X].
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For a discrete random variable,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(s)
=
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
P(X=k)s^k.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-ordinary-generating-function">
<title>PGF as an Ordinary Generating Function</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>p_k=P(X=k),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
p_0+p_1s+p_2s^2+\cdots.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the PGF is precisely the ordinary generating function of the
probability sequence</p>

<p>
<md>p_0,p_1,p_2,\ldots.</md>
</p>

<p>This connects directly with the generating-function methods developed
in Chapter 6.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-recover-probabilities-pgf">
<title>Recovering Probabilities from a PGF</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
p_ks^k,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
p_k
=
[s^k]G_X(s),
}</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>[s^k]</md>
</p>

<p>means <q>the coefficient of @@PROTECTED0@@.</q></p>

<p>Equivalently, if the PGF is sufficiently differentiable,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(X=k)
=
\frac{
G_X^{(k)}(0)
}{
k!
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-normalization">
<title>Normalization of a PGF</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(1)
=
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
P(X=k),</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(1)=1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(0)
=
P(X=0).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(0)=P(X=0).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-bernoulli">
<title>PGF of a Bernoulli Distribution</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p).</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
(1-p)s^0+ps^1.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(s)
=
1-p+ps.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-binomial">
<title>PGF of a Binomial Distribution</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bin}(n,p).</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
\sum_{k=0}^{n}
\binom nk
p^k(1-p)^{n-k}s^k.</md>
</p>

<p>By the binomial theorem,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(s)
=
(1-p+ps)^n.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-pgf-binomial-coefficient">
<title>Worked Example: Recovering a Binomial Probability</title>
<example>
<title>Extracting a Probability from a PGF</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bin}(4,p).</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
(1-p+ps)^4.</md>
</p>

<p>The probability</p>

<p>
<md>P(X=2)</md>
</p>

<p>is the coefficient of</p>

<p>
<md>s^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Expanding using the binomial theorem gives</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{answerbox}
\[
\boxed{
P(X=2)
=
\binom42
p^2(1-p)^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{answerbox}</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-geometric">
<title>PGF of a Geometric Distribution</title>
<p>Under the convention</p>

<p>
<md>X
=
\text{number of trials until first success},</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>P(X=k)
=
(1-p)^{k-1}p,
\qquad
k\geq1.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}
(1-p)^{k-1}ps^k.</md>
</p>

<p>Factor:</p>

<p>
<md>=
ps
\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}
[(1-p)s]^j.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(s)
=
\frac{
ps
}{
1-(1-p)s
},
}</md>
</p>

<p>for values of <m>s</m> where the series converges.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-poisson">
<title>PGF of a Poisson Distribution</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
e^{-\lambda}
\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}
s^k.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>=
e^{-\lambda}
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
\frac{(\lambda s)^k}{k!}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(s)
=
e^{\lambda(s-1)}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-derivatives">
<title>Derivatives of a PGF</title>
<p>Differentiate:</p>

<p>
<md>G_X'(s)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}
kP(X=k)s^{k-1}.</md>
</p>

<p>Setting</p>

<p>
<md>s=1</md>
</p>

<p>gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X'(1)
=
\mathbb{E}[X].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the first derivative produces the mean.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-second-pgf-derivative">
<title>Second PGF Derivative</title>
<p>Differentiate again:</p>

<p>
<md>G_X''(s)
=
\sum_{k=2}^{\infty}
k(k-1)
P(X=k)
s^{k-2}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X''(1)
=
\mathbb{E}[X(X-1)].
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is the second factorial moment.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-variance-from-pgf">
<title>Variance from a PGF</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>X^2
=
X(X-1)+X,</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X^2]
=
G_X''(1)+G_X'(1).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
G_X''(1)
+
G_X'(1)
-
[G_X'(1)]^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-poisson-pgf-moments">
<title>Worked Example: Poisson Moments from the PGF</title>
<example>
<title>Mean and Variance from a PGF</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
e^{\lambda(s-1)}.</md>
</p>

<p>Differentiate:</p>

<p>
<md>G_X'(s)
=
\lambda
e^{\lambda(s-1)}.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>G_X'(1)=\lambda.</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>G_X''(s)
=
\lambda^2
e^{\lambda(s-1)},</md>
</p>

<p>so</p>

<p>
<md>G_X''(1)=\lambda^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\lambda^2+\lambda-\lambda^2.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\lambda,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\lambda.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-independent-sums">
<title>PGFs of Independent Sums</title>
<p>Suppose <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent nonnegative integer-valued
random variables.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>G_{X+Y}(s)
=
\mathbb{E}[s^{X+Y}].</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>s^{X+Y}
=
s^Xs^Y,</md>
</p>

<p>independence gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_{X+Y}(s)
=
G_X(s)G_Y(s).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus convolution becomes multiplication.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-pgf-poisson-sum">
<title>Worked Example: Sum of Poisson Variables</title>
<example>
<title>Poisson Closure by PGFs</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda_1)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>Y\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda_2)</md>
</p>

<p>independently.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
e^{\lambda_1(s-1)}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>G_Y(s)
=
e^{\lambda_2(s-1)}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>G_{X+Y}(s)
=
e^{(\lambda_1+\lambda_2)(s-1)}.</md>
</p>

<p>This is the PGF of a Poisson distribution with parameter</p>

<p>
<md>\lambda_1+\lambda_2.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
X+Y
\sim
\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda_1+\lambda_2).
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-moment-generating-functions">
<title>Moment Generating Functions</title>
<p>Moment generating functions are useful when moments and sums are of
primary interest.</p>

<definition>
<title>Moment Generating Function</title>
<statement>
<p>The moment generating function, abbreviated MGF, of <m>X</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{tX}],
}</md>
</p>

<p>for those values of <m>t</m> where the expectation exists.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>At <m>t=0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(0)=1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-why-mgfs-generate-moments">
<title>Why MGFs Generate Moments</title>
<p>Expand</p>

<p>
<md>e^{tX}
=
1+tX+\frac{t^2X^2}{2!}+\cdots.</md>
</p>

<p>Formally taking expectations,</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
1
+
t\mathbb{E}[X]
+
\frac{t^2}{2!}\mathbb{E}[X^2]
+\cdots.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the coefficients contain the raw moments.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-derivatives">
<title>MGF Derivatives</title>
<p>When differentiation under the expectation is justified,</p>

<p>
<md>M_X'(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[Xe^{tX}].</md>
</p>

<p>At</p>

<p>
<md>t=0,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X'(0)
=
\mathbb{E}[X].
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X^{(k)}(0)
=
\mathbb{E}[X^k].
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-bernoulli">
<title>MGF of a Bernoulli Distribution</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
(1-p)e^0+pe^t.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
1-p+pe^t.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-binomial">
<title>MGF of a Binomial Distribution</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bin}(n,p),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
(1-p+pe^t)^n.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This can be derived either directly or by representing <m>X</m> as a sum
of independent Bernoulli variables.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-poisson">
<title>MGF of a Poisson Distribution</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
e^{tk}
e^{-\lambda}
\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
e^{-\lambda}
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
\frac{
(\lambda e^t)^k
}{
k!
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
\exp
\left(
\lambda(e^t-1)
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-normal">
<title>MGF of a Normal Distribution</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
\exp
\left(
\mu t
+
\frac{\sigma^2t^2}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>

<p>For the standard normal,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_Z(t)
=
e^{t^2/2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-normal-mgf-moments">
<title>Worked Example: Normal Moments from the MGF</title>
<example>
<title>Recovering Mean and Variance</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2).</md>
</p>

<p>Its MGF is</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
\exp
\left(
\mu t+\frac{\sigma^2t^2}{2}
\right).</md>
</p>

<p>Differentiate:</p>

<p>
<md>M_X'(t)
=
(\mu+\sigma^2t)
M_X(t).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>M_X'(0)=\mu.</md>
</p>

<p>A second differentiation gives</p>

<p>
<md>M_X''(0)
=
\mu^2+\sigma^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
M_X''(0)
-
[M_X'(0)]^2.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]=\mu,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(X)=\sigma^2.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-exponential">
<title>MGF of an Exponential Distribution</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
\int_0^\infty
e^{tx}
\lambda e^{-\lambda x}
\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>=
\lambda
\int_0^\infty
e^{-(\lambda-t)x}
\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>This converges when</p>

<p>
<md>t&lt;\lambda.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda-t},
\qquad
t&lt;\lambda.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-gamma">
<title>MGF of a Gamma Distribution</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha,\lambda)</md>
</p>

<p>using the shape-rate parameterization,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
\left(
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda-t}
\right)^\alpha,
\qquad
t&lt;\lambda.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Setting</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha=1</md>
</p>

<p>recovers the exponential MGF.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-chi-square">
<title>MGF of a Chi-Square Distribution</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>\chi^2_\nu
=
\operatorname{Gamma}
\left(
\frac{\nu}{2},
\frac12
\right),</md>
</p>

<p>its MGF is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
(1-2t)^{-\nu/2},
\qquad
t&lt;\frac12.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-independent-sums">
<title>MGFs of Independent Sums</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent and their MGFs exist, then</p>

<p>
<md>M_{X+Y}(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{t(X+Y)}].</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>e^{t(X+Y)}
=
e^{tX}e^{tY},</md>
</p>

<p>independence gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_{X+Y}(t)
=
M_X(t)M_Y(t).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-mgf-normal-sum">
<title>Worked Example: Sum of Independent Normals</title>
<example>
<title>Normal Closure by MGFs</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim N(\mu_1,\sigma_1^2)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>Y\sim N(\mu_2,\sigma_2^2)</md>
</p>

<p>independently.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>M_{X+Y}(t)
=
M_X(t)M_Y(t).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>=
\exp
\left(
\mu_1t+\frac{\sigma_1^2t^2}{2}
\right)
\exp
\left(
\mu_2t+\frac{\sigma_2^2t^2}{2}
\right).</md>
</p>

<p>Combining exponents,</p>

<p>
<md>=
\exp
\left[
(\mu_1+\mu_2)t
+
\frac{
(\sigma_1^2+\sigma_2^2)t^2
}{2}
\right].</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
X+Y
\sim
N
\left(
\mu_1+\mu_2,
\sigma_1^2+\sigma_2^2
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-uniqueness">
<title>MGF Uniqueness</title>
<p>Under suitable conditions, if two random variables have the same MGF in
an open interval containing <m>0</m>, then they have the same distribution.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)=M_Y(t)
\text{ near }0
\Longrightarrow
X\overset{d}{=}Y.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This allows transforms to identify distributions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-not-always-exist">
<title>MGFs Do Not Always Exist</title>
<p>An MGF may fail to exist for nonzero values of <m>t</m>.</p>

<p>Heavy-tailed distributions provide important examples.</p>

<p>For instance, the standard Cauchy distribution does not have a finite
MGF in a neighborhood of zero.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{MGFs are powerful but not universal}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This motivates characteristic functions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-characteristic-functions">
<title>Characteristic Functions</title>
<definition>
<title>Characteristic Function</title>
<statement>
<p>The characteristic function of a random variable <m>X</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{itX}],
\qquad
t\in\mathbb{R},
}</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>i^2=-1.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The Greek letter</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi</md>
</p>

<p>is called <q>varphi</q> or <q>phi.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-euler-characteristic">
<title>Euler's Formula and Characteristic Functions</title>
<p>Euler's formula gives</p>

<p>
<md>e^{itX}
=
\cos(tX)
+
i\sin(tX).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[\cos(tX)]
+
i\mathbb{E}[\sin(tX)].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus a characteristic function combines cosine and sine transforms of
the distribution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-characteristic-functions-exist">
<title>Characteristic Functions Always Exist</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>|e^{itX}|=1,</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>\left|
\mathbb{E}[e^{itX}]
\right|
\leq1.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|\varphi_X(t)|\leq1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Unlike MGFs, characteristic functions exist for every real-valued random
variable.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-characteristic-basic-properties">
<title>Basic Characteristic Function Properties</title>
<p>Every characteristic function satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(0)=1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|\varphi_X(t)|\leq1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Furthermore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(-t)
=
\overline{\varphi_X(t)},
}</md>
</p>

<p>where the overline denotes complex conjugation.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-bernoulli">
<title>Characteristic Function of a Bernoulli Variable</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_X(t)
=
(1-p)+pe^{it}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
1-p+pe^{it}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-binomial">
<title>Characteristic Function of a Binomial Variable</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bin}(n,p),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
(1-p+pe^{it})^n.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is obtained from the MGF by formally replacing <m>t</m> with <m>it</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-poisson">
<title>Characteristic Function of a Poisson Variable</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
\exp
\left(
\lambda(e^{it}-1)
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-normal">
<title>Characteristic Function of a Normal Variable</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
\exp
\left(
i\mu t
-
\frac{\sigma^2t^2}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>

<p>For the standard normal,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_Z(t)
=
e^{-t^2/2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-cauchy">
<title>Characteristic Function of a Cauchy Variable</title>
<p>For a standard Cauchy random variable,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
e^{-|t|}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This characteristic function exists even though the Cauchy MGF does
not.</p>

<p>This illustrates one major advantage of characteristic functions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-independent-sums">
<title>Characteristic Functions of Independent Sums</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent, then</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_{X+Y}(t)
=
\mathbb{E}
[
e^{it(X+Y)}
].</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>e^{it(X+Y)}
=
e^{itX}e^{itY},</md>
</p>

<p>independence gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_{X+Y}(t)
=
\varphi_X(t)\varphi_Y(t).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus characteristic functions convert convolution into multiplication.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-uniqueness">
<title>Characteristic Function Uniqueness</title>
<p>Characteristic functions uniquely determine probability distributions.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
\varphi_Y(t)
\text{ for all }t
\Longrightarrow
X\overset{d}{=}Y.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Unlike MGF uniqueness, this result does not require existence in a
neighborhood of zero because characteristic functions always exist.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-moments">
<title>Characteristic Functions and Moments</title>
<p>If sufficiently many moments exist, derivatives of the characteristic
function recover them.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_X'(0)
=
i\mathbb{E}[X].</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X^{(k)}(0)
=
i^k
\mathbb{E}[X^k].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X^k]
=
\frac{
\varphi_X^{(k)}(0)
}{
i^k
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-fourier-analysis">
<title>Characteristic Functions and Fourier Analysis</title>
<p>For a continuous random variable with density <m>f_X</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_X(t)
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
e^{itx}
f_X(x)\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the characteristic function is essentially a Fourier transform of
the probability density.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{characteristic functions}
\longleftrightarrow
\text{Fourier analysis}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-fourier-inversion-probability">
<title>Fourier Inversion Preview</title>
<p>Under appropriate regularity conditions, the density can be recovered
from the characteristic function through Fourier inversion:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f_X(x)
=
\frac1{2\pi}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
e^{-itx}
\varphi_X(t)\,dt.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus transform and distribution can be viewed as two representations of
the same probability law.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cf-convergence-preview">
<title>Characteristic Functions and Convergence</title>
<p>Characteristic functions are especially important in limit theorems.</p>

<p>A major result states, roughly, that convergence of characteristic
functions to a valid limiting characteristic function corresponds to
convergence in distribution.</p>

<p>This principle is formalized by Lévy's continuity theorem.</p>

<p>It will be used conceptually in the study of central limit theorems.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-levy-continuity-preview">
<title>Lévy Continuity Theorem Preview</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_{X_n}(t)
\to
\varphi(t)</md>
</p>

<p>for every real <m>t</m>, and suppose <m>\varphi</m> is continuous at <m>0</m> and
is the characteristic function of some random variable <m>X</m>.</p>

<p>Then, under the standard theorem,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
X_n
\xrightarrow{d}
X.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\xrightarrow{d}</md>
</p>

<p>means <q>converges in distribution.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-laplace-transforms-probability">
<title>Laplace Transforms</title>
<p>For nonnegative random variables, Laplace transforms are often more
natural than MGFs.</p>

<definition>
<title>Laplace Transform of a Random Variable</title>
<statement>
<p>For a nonnegative random variable <m>X</m>, define</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_X(s)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{-sX}],
\qquad
s\geq0.
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For a continuous variable,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_X(s)
=
\int_0^\infty
e^{-sx}f_X(x)\,dx.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-laplace-mgf-relation">
<title>Relation Between Laplace Transform and MGF</title>
<p>Whenever both expressions are defined,</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{tX}]</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>L_X(s)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{-sX}].</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_X(s)
=
M_X(-s).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the Laplace transform is the MGF evaluated at a negative argument.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-laplace-exponential">
<title>Laplace Transform of an Exponential Variable</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>L_X(s)
=
\int_0^\infty
e^{-sx}
\lambda e^{-\lambda x}
\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>=
\lambda
\int_0^\infty
e^{-(\lambda+s)x}
\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_X(s)
=
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda+s}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-laplace-gamma">
<title>Laplace Transform of a Gamma Variable</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha,\lambda)</md>
</p>

<p>under the shape-rate convention, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_X(s)
=
\left(
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda+s}
\right)^\alpha.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-laplace-independent-sums">
<title>Laplace Transforms of Independent Sums</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent nonnegative random variables, then</p>

<p>
<md>L_{X+Y}(s)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{-s(X+Y)}].</md>
</p>

<p>Independence gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_{X+Y}(s)
=
L_X(s)L_Y(s).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Again, convolution becomes multiplication.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-laplace-gamma-sum">
<title>Worked Example: Gamma Closure</title>
<example>
<title>Sum of Independent Gamma Variables</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha_1,\lambda)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>Y\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha_2,\lambda)</md>
</p>

<p>independently.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>L_X(s)
=
\left(
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda+s}
\right)^{\alpha_1}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>L_Y(s)
=
\left(
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda+s}
\right)^{\alpha_2}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>L_{X+Y}(s)
=
\left(
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda+s}
\right)^{\alpha_1+\alpha_2}.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
X+Y
\sim
\operatorname{Gamma}
(\alpha_1+\alpha_2,\lambda).
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cumulant-generating-functions">
<title>Cumulant Generating Functions</title>
<p>The logarithm of the MGF produces another important transform.</p>

<definition>
<title>Cumulant Generating Function</title>
<statement>
<p>If <m>M_X(t)</m> exists, the cumulant generating function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_X(t)
=
\ln M_X(t).
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Its derivatives at zero are called cumulants.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cumulants">
<title>Cumulants</title>
<p>The <m>k</m>-th cumulant is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_k
=
K_X^{(k)}(0),
}</md>
</p>

<p>when the derivative exists.</p>

<p>The first few cumulants have familiar meanings.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-first-cumulant">
<title>First Cumulant</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>K_X(t)
=
\ln M_X(t),</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>K_X'(t)
=
\frac{
M_X'(t)
}{
M_X(t)
}.</md>
</p>

<p>At <m>t=0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(0)=1,</md>
</p>

<p>so</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_1
=
\mathbb{E}[X].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the first cumulant is the mean.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-second-cumulant">
<title>Second Cumulant</title>
<p>Differentiating again yields</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_2
=
\operatorname{Var}(X).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the second cumulant is the variance.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-higher-cumulants">
<title>Third and Fourth Cumulants</title>
<p>The third cumulant is the third central moment:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_3
=
\mathbb{E}
[
(X-\mu)^3
].
}</md>
</p>

<p>The fourth cumulant is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_4
=
\mathbb{E}
[
(X-\mu)^4
]
-
3\sigma^4.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the fourth cumulant measures excess fourth-order behavior relative
to the normal distribution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-cumulants-independent-sums">
<title>Cumulants of Independent Sums</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent, then</p>

<p>
<md>M_{X+Y}(t)
=
M_X(t)M_Y(t).</md>
</p>

<p>Taking logarithms,</p>

<p>
<md>K_{X+Y}(t)
=
K_X(t)+K_Y(t).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_k(X+Y)
=
\kappa_k(X)
+
\kappa_k(Y).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Cumulants add under independent sums.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-poisson-cumulants">
<title>Worked Example: Poisson Cumulants</title>
<example>
<title>All Poisson Cumulants</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>the MGF is</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
\exp
\left(
\lambda(e^t-1)
\right).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>K_X(t)
=
\lambda(e^t-1).</md>
</p>

<p>Every derivative of <m>K_X</m> at <m>0</m> equals <m>\lambda</m>.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_k
=
\lambda
}</md>
</p>

<p>for every positive integer <m>k</m>.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-normal-cumulants">
<title>Cumulants of a Normal Distribution</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
\exp
\left(
\mu t+\frac{\sigma^2t^2}{2}
\right).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>K_X(t)
=
\mu t
+
\frac{\sigma^2t^2}{2}.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_1=\mu,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_2=\sigma^2,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_k=0
\quad
\text{for }k\geq3.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The vanishing of all higher cumulants is a defining structural feature
of the normal distribution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-scaling-cumulants">
<title>Scaling Cumulants</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>Y=aX+b,</md>
</p>

<p>the cumulants transform as</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_1(Y)
=
a\kappa_1(X)+b,
}</md>
</p>

<p>while for</p>

<p>
<md>k\geq2,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_k(Y)
=
a^k\kappa_k(X).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus translation affects only the first cumulant.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transforms-and-convolution">
<title>Transforms and Convolution</title>
<p>Convolution appears naturally when independent variables are added.</p>

<p>In the original distribution domain:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
f_{X+Y}
=
f_X*f_Y.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In the transform domain:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
T_{X+Y}
=
T_XT_Y.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore transforms convert a convolution problem into multiplication.</p>

<p>This is one of their most important advantages.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-probability-transform-table">
<title>Transform Table</title>
<p>
<md>\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{Transform}
&amp;
\text{Definition}
&amp;
\text{Typical Setting}
\\
\hline
G_X(s)
&amp;
\mathbb{E}[s^X]
&amp;
\text{nonnegative integer-valued }X
\\[1mm]
M_X(t)
&amp;
\mathbb{E}[e^{tX}]
&amp;
\text{moments, sums}
\\[1mm]
\varphi_X(t)
&amp;
\mathbb{E}[e^{itX}]
&amp;
\text{general distributions}
\\[1mm]
L_X(s)
&amp;
\mathbb{E}[e^{-sX}]
&amp;
\text{nonnegative }X
\\[1mm]
K_X(t)
&amp;
\ln M_X(t)
&amp;
\text{cumulants}
\end{array}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-mgf-relation">
<title>Relationship Between PGF and MGF</title>
<p>For a nonnegative integer-valued <m>X</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{tX}].</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>e^{tX}
=
(e^t)^X,</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
G_X(e^t).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the MGF can be obtained from the PGF by substituting</p>

<p>
<md>s=e^t.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-mgf-cf-relation">
<title>Relationship Between MGF and Characteristic Function</title>
<p>Whenever the MGF has an appropriate complex extension,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
M_X(it).
}</md>
</p>

<p>The characteristic function replaces the real exponential factor with
a complex oscillatory factor.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transforms-linear-transformations">
<title>Transforms of Linear Transformations</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>Y=aX+b,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>M_Y(t)
=
\mathbb{E}
[
e^{t(aX+b)}
].</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_Y(t)
=
e^{bt}
M_X(at).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_Y(t)
=
e^{ibt}
\varphi_X(at).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-cf-standardization">
<title>Worked Example: Standardization via Characteristic Functions</title>
<example>
<title>Standardizing a Normal Variable</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2)</md>
</p>

<p>and define</p>

<p>
<md>Z
=
\frac{X-\mu}{\sigma}.</md>
</p>

<p>Using the linear-transform rule,</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_Z(t)
=
e^{-it\mu/\sigma}
\varphi_X(t/\sigma).</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_X(u)
=
\exp
\left(
i\mu u
-
\frac{\sigma^2u^2}{2}
\right),</md>
</p>

<p>substituting</p>

<p>
<md>u=\frac{t}{\sigma}</md>
</p>

<p>gives</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_Z(t)
=
e^{-t^2/2}.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
Z\sim N(0,1).
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transform-distribution-identification">
<title>Transforms and Distribution Identification</title>
<p>A common strategy is:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>compute the transform of a random variable;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>simplify the result;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize it as the transform of a known distribution;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use uniqueness to identify the distribution.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<p>This approach is especially useful for sums of independent variables.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-worked-chi-square-transform">
<title>Worked Example: Chi-Square Additivity</title>
<example>
<title>Adding Independent Chi-Square Variables</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\chi^2_{\nu_1}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>Y\sim\chi^2_{\nu_2}</md>
</p>

<p>independently.</p>

<p>Their MGFs are</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
(1-2t)^{-\nu_1/2}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>M_Y(t)
=
(1-2t)^{-\nu_2/2}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>M_{X+Y}(t)
=
(1-2t)^{-(\nu_1+\nu_2)/2}.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
X+Y
\sim
\chi^2_{\nu_1+\nu_2}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transform-inversion">
<title>Transform Inversion</title>
<p>A transform compresses a probability distribution into another
functional representation.</p>

<p>To recover the original distribution, an inversion procedure may be
used.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{coefficient extraction for PGFs},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Fourier inversion for characteristic functions},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Laplace inversion for Laplace transforms}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus probability transforms are reversible under appropriate
conditions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transforms-recurrence-relations">
<title>Transforms and Recurrence Relations</title>
<p>PGFs are also useful when probabilities satisfy recurrence relations.</p>

<p>Suppose a sequence</p>

<p>
<md>p_0,p_1,p_2,\ldots</md>
</p>

<p>satisfies a recurrence.</p>

<p>Multiplying by powers of <m>s</m> and summing may convert the recurrence
into an algebraic equation for</p>

<p>
<md>G(s).</md>
</p>

<p>This is the same principle used for generating functions in
combinatorics.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transforms-random-sums">
<title>Transforms and Random Sums</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>N</md>
</p>

<p>is a nonnegative integer-valued random variable and</p>

<p>
<md>X_1,X_2,\ldots</md>
</p>

<p>are i.i.d. random variables independent of <m>N</m>.</p>

<p>Define the random sum</p>

<p>
<md>S_N
=
X_1+\cdots+X_N.</md>
</p>

<p>Transforms can encode the distribution of this compound random quantity.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-pgf-random-sum">
<title>PGF of a Random Sum</title>
<p>Suppose the <m>X_i</m> are nonnegative integer-valued with common PGF</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s).</md>
</p>

<p>Conditioning on <m>N=n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>G_{S_N}(s\mid N=n)
=
[G_X(s)]^n.</md>
</p>

<p>Averaging over <m>N</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_{S_N}(s)
=
G_N(G_X(s)).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus random summation becomes composition of generating functions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-compound-poisson-transform-preview">
<title>Compound Poisson Distribution Preview</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>N\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>S_N
=
X_1+\cdots+X_N.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>G_N(s)
=
e^{\lambda(s-1)}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_{S_N}(s)
=
\exp
\left(
\lambda(G_X(s)-1)
\right).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is the PGF of a compound Poisson distribution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transforms-branching-processes">
<title>Transforms and Branching Processes Preview</title>
<p>PGFs are fundamental in branching processes.</p>

<p>If each individual produces a random number of offspring with PGF</p>

<p>
<md>G(s),</md>
</p>

<p>then composition of <m>G</m> describes offspring counts across
generations.</p>

<p>For example, the PGF after two generations often involves</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G(G(s)).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus functional iteration encodes population evolution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transforms-queueing-preview">
<title>Transforms and Queueing Theory Preview</title>
<p>Laplace transforms and PGFs occur frequently in queueing theory.</p>

<p>They are used to describe:</p>

<p>
<md>\text{waiting times},</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\text{service times},</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\text{queue lengths},</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\text{arrival counts}.</md>
</p>

<p>Transform equations can sometimes replace difficult integral or
recurrence equations.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transforms-reliability-preview">
<title>Transforms and Reliability Preview</title>
<p>Laplace transforms are useful for sums of independent lifetime and
waiting-time variables.</p>

<p>For example, if a system lifetime is a sum of independent stages,</p>

<p>
<md>T=T_1+\cdots+T_n,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_T(s)
=
\prod_{i=1}^{n}
L_{T_i}(s).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This may be easier to analyze than repeated convolution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-transforms-limit-theorems">
<title>Transforms and Limit Theorems</title>
<p>Probability transforms are especially powerful when analyzing long
sums.</p>

<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>S_n=X_1+\cdots+X_n.</md>
</p>

<p>For independent variables,</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_{S_n}(t)
=
\prod_{j=1}^{n}
\varphi_{X_j}(t).</md>
</p>

<p>Products become easier to analyze after logarithms:</p>

<p>
<md>\ln\varphi_{S_n}(t)
=
\sum_{j=1}^{n}
\ln\varphi_{X_j}(t).</md>
</p>

<p>This is one reason cumulants and characteristic functions are natural
tools for central limit theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-small-t-characteristic-expansion">
<title>Small-<m>t</m> Expansion of a Characteristic Function</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> has finite second moment, then near <m>t=0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi_X(t)
=
1
+
it\mathbb{E}[X]
-
\frac{t^2}{2}
\mathbb{E}[X^2]
+
o(t^2).</md>
</p>

<p>For a centered variable with</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]=0,</md>
</p>

<p>this becomes</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
1
-
\frac{\sigma^2t^2}{2}
+
o(t^2).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This expansion underlies transform proofs of the central limit theorem.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-moments-versus-transforms">
<title>Moments Versus Transforms</title>
<p>A sequence of moments may describe a distribution, but not every
distribution is uniquely determined by its moments without additional
conditions.</p>

<p>Transforms often encode more complete information.</p>

<p>In particular,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{characteristic function}
}</md>
</p>

<p>always uniquely determines the probability distribution.</p>

<p>This makes characteristic functions more robust than relying only on
moments.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-moment-determinacy-preview">
<title>Moment Determinacy Preview</title>
<p>A distribution is called moment-determinate if its sequence of moments
uniquely determines the distribution.</p>

<p>Some distributions are moment-indeterminate.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{same moments}
\not\Longrightarrow
\text{same distribution}
}</md>
</p>

<p>in complete generality.</p>

<p>Conditions such as Carleman's criterion provide sufficient conditions
for moment determinacy.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-multivariate-mgf">
<title>Multivariate Moment Generating Functions</title>
<p>For a random vector</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{X}
=
(X_1,\ldots,X_n)^T,</md>
</p>

<p>define</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{t})
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
e^{\mathbf{t}^T\mathbf{X}}
\right].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{t}
=
(t_1,\ldots,t_n)^T.</md>
</p>

<p>Mixed partial derivatives recover joint moments.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-multivariate-characteristic-function">
<title>Multivariate Characteristic Functions</title>
<p>For a random vector <m>\mathbf{X}</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_{\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{t})
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
e^{i\mathbf{t}^T\mathbf{X}}
\right].
}</md>
</p>

<p>This function uniquely determines the joint distribution of
<m>\mathbf{X}</m>.</p>

<p>Thus characteristic functions extend naturally to multivariate
probability.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-independence-multivariate-transforms">
<title>Independence and Multivariate Transforms</title>
<p>Random variables</p>

<p>
<md>X_1,\ldots,X_n</md>
</p>

<p>are independent if their joint characteristic function factors:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_{X_1,\ldots,X_n}
(t_1,\ldots,t_n)
=
\prod_{j=1}^{n}
\varphi_{X_j}(t_j).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This provides a transform-based characterization of independence.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-probability-transform-strategy">
<title>Transform Strategy</title>
<p>When considering a probability transform, ask:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>Is the variable nonnegative integer-valued?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Do I need coefficients or factorial moments?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Does the MGF exist near zero?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Would a characteristic function be safer?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Is the variable nonnegative, making a Laplace transform natural?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Am I adding independent random variables?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Would a logarithm simplify multiplication into addition?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Do I need moment information or the full distribution?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Is an inversion formula necessary?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Is the goal a limit theorem or asymptotic approximation?</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-probability-transforms-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-pgf-and-mgf">
<title>Confusing PGF and MGF</title>
<p>The PGF is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X(s)
=
\mathbb{E}[s^X].
}</md>
</p>

<p>The MGF is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{tX}].
}</md>
</p>

<p>They are different transforms.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-pgfs-for-arbitrary-real-valued-variables">
<title>Using PGFs for Arbitrary Real-Valued Variables</title>
<p>PGFs are naturally suited to nonnegative integer-valued random
variables.</p>

<p>General real-valued variables are better handled by MGFs,
characteristic functions, or other transforms.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-an-mgf-always-exists">
<title>Assuming an MGF Always Exists</title>
<p>MGFs may diverge for every nonzero <m>t</m> near zero.</p>

<p>Characteristic functions always exist.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-the-domain-of-an-mgf">
<title>Forgetting the Domain of an MGF</title>
<p>For example, if</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>M_X(t)
=
\frac{\lambda}{\lambda-t}</md>
</p>

<p>only for</p>

<p>
<md>t&lt;\lambda.</md>
</p>

<p>The convergence domain matters.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-independence-when-multiplying-transforms">
<title>Forgetting Independence When Multiplying Transforms</title>
<p>The identity</p>

<p>
<md>M_{X+Y}(t)
=
M_X(t)M_Y(t)</md>
</p>

<p>requires independence.</p>

<p>The same warning applies to PGFs, Laplace transforms, and characteristic
functions of sums.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-raw-moments-and-factorial-moments">
<title>Confusing Raw Moments and Factorial Moments</title>
<p>PGF derivatives naturally give factorial moments:</p>

<p>
<md>G_X''(1)
=
\mathbb{E}[X(X-1)].</md>
</p>

<p>MGF derivatives give raw moments:</p>

<p>
<md>M_X''(0)
=
\mathbb{E}[X^2].</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-the-complex-unit-in-a-characteristic-function">
<title>Forgetting the Complex Unit in a Characteristic Function</title>
<p>The characteristic function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{itX}],
}</md>
</p>

<p>not</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[e^{tX}].</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-characteristic-functions-are-real-valued">
<title>Assuming Characteristic Functions Are Real-Valued</title>
<p>Characteristic functions may be complex-valued.</p>

<p>For symmetric distributions centered at zero, they are often real, but
this is not true in general.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-laplace-transform-sign-convention">
<title>Confusing Laplace Transform Sign Convention</title>
<p>A probability Laplace transform is commonly written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L_X(s)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{-sX}].
}</md>
</p>

<p>The negative sign distinguishes it from the MGF.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-differentiating-without-checking-moment-existence">
<title>Differentiating Without Checking Moment Existence</title>
<p>Formal derivatives of a transform correspond to moments only when the
required interchange of differentiation and expectation is justified.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-equal-moments-always-imply-equal-distributions">
<title>Assuming Equal Moments Always Imply Equal Distributions</title>
<p>Moment sequences do not uniquely determine every probability
distribution.</p>

<p>Characteristic functions, however, do uniquely determine probability
laws.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-probability-transforms-applications">
<title>Applications</title>
<p>\begin{applicationbox}</p>

<p><em>Distribution Sums.</em>
Transforms replace convolution by multiplication for independent sums.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Combinatorics.</em>
PGFs connect probability sequences with ordinary generating functions.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Statistics.</em>
MGFs and characteristic functions are used to derive sampling
distributions and asymptotic results.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Central Limit Theory.</em>
Characteristic functions provide one of the standard proof methods for
central limit theorems.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Queueing Theory.</em>
PGFs and Laplace transforms describe queue lengths, service times, and
waiting times.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Reliability Theory.</em>
Laplace transforms simplify sums of independent lifetime stages.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Stochastic Processes.</em>
Transform methods are fundamental for Poisson processes, branching
processes, and Markov models.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Signal Processing.</em>
Characteristic functions connect probability distributions with Fourier
analysis.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Compound Distributions.</em>
Transform composition provides a natural description of random sums and
compound Poisson models.</p>

<p>\end{applicationbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-08-probability-transforms-subsec-probability-transforms-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the probability generating function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For a PGF <m>G_X</m>, what is</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(1)?</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(0)=P(X=0).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the moment generating function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the characteristic function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the Laplace transform of a nonnegative random variable.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Define the cumulant generating function.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the relationship between the PGF and MGF for a nonnegative
integer-valued random variable.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the product rule for PGFs of independent sums.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the product rule for MGFs of independent sums.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the PGF of</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the PGF of</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bin}(n,p).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the PGF of</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
